<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#land-tenure">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Land tenure</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as land grant system</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Land tenure refers to the rules and arrangements by which land is held, used, granted and transferred. In many empires, grants of land or of the revenue from land were used to reward soldiers, officials and religious institutions in return for service. The site discusses such systems in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a>, where they shaped fiscal and military organisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/land-tenure.jpg" alt="Land tenure" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene23_Harold_sacramentum_fecit_Willelmo_duci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cecil Rhodes, prime minister from 1890 to 1896, raised the property qualification for voters through the Franchise and Ballot Act of 1892 and introduced the Glen Grey Act of 1894, which created individual <b>land tenure</b> and local councils in African areas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The hakura system governed <b>land tenure</b> and social hierarchy alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Haile Selassie's attempts to reform <b>land tenure</b> after 1930 repeatedly stalled against noble resistance, and the unreformed system produced the rural poverty and elite resentment that enabled the Derg's seizure of power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The timar system, the empire’s basic agrarian-military institution, assigned revenues from specific agricultural lands to sipahi cavalrymen in exchange for military service, so that <b>land tenure</b>, taxation and military organisation were joined in a single mechanism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The state kept tight control over agricultural production, <b>land tenure</b> and taxation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Remnants lasted far longer: the last seigneurial rents in Quebec were paid in 1970, Scotland ended feudal <b>land tenure</b> only in 2004, and Sark replaced the tenants’ seats in its parliament, the Chief Pleas, with elected members in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
